{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC segmenting Markdown to wiki renderer (pure capability)\n\n## Problem\nJira Data Center stores descriptions as lossy wiki markup. WikiTextCodec currently sends wiki strings without Markdown rendering, so raw Markdown appears literally. Whole-body pandoc conversion is unsafe for punctuation-dense prose, regular expressions, HTML-dense text, and ASCII tables. The corpus experiment measured that a segmenting renderer can enrich about 68 percent of bodies and 78 percent of characters while preserving dangerous units.\n\nThis story adds only a pure renderer module. It does not wire the renderer into WikiTextCodec, OutboundFieldMapper, CREATE, or any live send site. Production cutover remains in disparaged-parodistic-shrew, together with echo suppression, rollback, and second-pass convergence.\n\n## Existing contracts\n- RichTextCodec and WikiTextCodec live in adapters/jira_family/rich_text.py.\n- WikiTextCodec.fit_outbound is not identity: it truncates strings above 32,767 characters and appends the established truncation suffix. That behavior and its tests remain unchanged.\n- WikiTextCodec.normalize_outbound and to_wire remain identity on strings; decode_inbound remains identity on strings and maps None to an empty string.\n- The codec law normalize_outbound(t) == decode_inbound(to_wire(t)) remains green unchanged.\n- rebar_reconciler/inbound_fields.py owns normalize_rich_text, the shared inbound decoder; it remains unchanged.\n- ADR 0083 defines the vendor adapter import boundary; jira_family must not import Cloud-specific adapter modules.\n\n## Dependency and optionality\nAdd an optional project extra named jira-dc containing the exact pin pypandoc-binary==1.17. The core dependency list remains unchanged. Add the pypandoc probe and capability hint to src/rebar/_optional.py, and import it lazily at the renderer boundary with guard_import. The renderer catches OptionalDependencyError and conversion failures, returns the exact original input, and emits the fallback warning once per process. No import of pypandoc occurs during import rebar.\n\nInclude jira-dc in the dev self-reference so make install and Gerrit CI install the real wheel and bundled pandoc executable. Regenerate uv.lock. A non-skipping test imports pypandoc, asserts the bundled executable is available, and performs a real commonmark to jira conversion; absence of the dependency or Jira writer fails the gating suite rather than selecting the fallback path. A separate monkeypatched unit test covers the fallback.\n\nTimeout and process hardening plus GPL/SBOM work remain owned by ashy-waterlog-hamadryas.\n\n## Renderer\nAdd adapters/jira_family/wiki_render.py with:\n\n    render_markdown_to_wiki(markdown: str) -> str\n\nSplit the body into ordered fenced-code blocks, headings, block quotes, blank-delimited paragraphs, and individual list items. Classification is deterministic and precedence ordered:\n\n1. Existing {noformat} blocks and fenced code blocks are protected as complete units.\n2. ASCII tables are runs of at least two pipe-bearing lines whose delimiter row consists only of pipes, colons, hyphens, and whitespace; wrap the exact run once in {noformat}.\n3. HTML-dense units contain an HTML start/end/self-closing tag and pass through byte-identically.\n4. Regex-heavy units have a regex delimiter or metacharacter run outside inline-code spans and pass through byte-identically.\n5. Remaining headings, quotes, list items, and prose paragraphs are safe and go to pandoc.\n\nPreserve existing {noformat} blocks byte-identically so repeated rendering never nests them. Preserve block order and blank-line separation exactly when reassembling.\n\nApply prose-arrow substitutions (->, <-, <->, => to their Unicode forms) to safe Markdown before pandoc. A scanner excludes fenced code and backtick-delimited inline-code spans, so code such as obj->method() stays byte-identical.\n\nRender safe units with the real pypandoc conversion using input commonmark, output jira, and --wrap=none. The verified checklist behavior for \"- [ ] todo\" is the exact Jira output \"* \\\\[ ] todo\"; the happy path asserts that escaped checkbox text rather than assuming a native Jira task-list construct. A fenced python block becomes {code:python}...{code}; a heading becomes h1. with the pandoc Jira anchor prefix accepted as renderer output.\n\n## Hermetic corpus\nCommit sanitized JSON fixtures under tests/fixtures/dc_wiki_corpus/. They are produced from the established ticket corpus experiment and are never fetched at test time. The fixtures include 179 code-arrow bodies and 70 table-erosion bodies, including the 9115 to 9110 ratchet example. Each fixture identifies protected excerpts and safe renderable excerpts explicitly, so the oracle does not infer correctness from unchanged fallback output.\n\n## Tests\nHappy path uses real pypandoc: a heading, checklist, and fenced code render through render_markdown_to_wiki into the measured h1 anchor form, \"* \\\\[ ] todo\", and {code:python} in original order.\n\nHeld-out edges:\n- A mixed body wraps an ASCII table once in {noformat}, converts a prose arrow, preserves an inline-code arrow, and leaves regex/HTML-dense blocks unchanged.\n- Existing {noformat} content stays byte-identical through repeated rendering.\n- All 179 code-arrow fixtures retain every declared protected code excerpt while each declared safe prose-arrow excerpt converts.\n- All 70 erosion fixtures preserve every declared table byte through five renderer passes, including the known ratchet body.\n- A missing import or raised conversion returns the exact input and emits only one fallback warning per process.\n- The real-pandoc corpus test requires at least one safe unit in every enrichment-eligible fixture, asserts the output differs from input for that unit, calculates the exact rendered-body and rendered-character ratios, and requires both recorded floors. It also compares every declared protected excerpt byte-for-byte and rejects loss, duplication, or reordering of non-rendered units. Therefore identity fallback cannot make the corpus test green.\n\nExisting WikiTextCodec truncation, identity, and codec-law tests remain unmodified and green. No contention test is required because this leaf is a pure transformation plus one process boundary, with no store operations, retry/backoff, or cached store state.\n\n## Surface parity census\n- CLI command: out of scope; the pure renderer has no command surface.\n- MCP/API tool: out of scope; no tool exposes this internal capability.\n- Library facade: out of scope; no public rebar import is added.\n- Automated callers: out of scope in this leaf; disparaged-parodistic-shrew owns production wiring and end-to-end caller coverage.\n- Dual direction: out of scope by design; this capability is one-way and the cutover story owns synchronization convergence.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Safe units render through real pypandoc in original order, proven by the non-skipping happy-path unit test.\n- [ ] Regex-heavy and HTML-dense units pass through byte-identically, proven by the mixed-body held-out test.\n- [ ] Prose arrows convert before pandoc while fenced and inline-code arrows stay byte-identical.\n- [ ] Every detected ASCII table is wrapped once in {noformat}; existing {noformat} blocks never nest.\n- [ ] All 179 code-arrow fixtures preserve declared protected excerpts and convert declared safe excerpts.\n- [ ] All 70 erosion fixtures preserve exact declared table bytes over five passes, including the ratchet body.\n- [ ] jira-dc pins pypandoc-binary==1.17, dev/CI installs it, and a gating test hard-fails if real pandoc Jira rendering is unavailable.\n- [ ] Missing pypandoc or conversion failure returns the exact input and logs one fallback warning per process.\n- [ ] The vendored corpus reaches both enrichment floors with non-vacuous conversion and zero declared protected-excerpt corruption.\n- [ ] WikiTextCodec.fit_outbound truncation and the remaining codec contracts stay unchanged, proven by existing tests."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786568941563126001,"uuid":"982ac1a2-6b7d-47e3-a6aa-c1c38b176734"}